From 5228ebeb60cc8f55d6e335ec69026108e72e9b14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 15:30:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give a captured numeric local a raw double cell, item 3-7
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A numeric local a nested function names kept its JSVariable, on the grounds
that a closure captures through a cell. It does, and the cell already exists:
the expression compiler rewrites any CLR local a nested lambda references into
a Box<T>, and Box<double> IS the shared cell — one allocation where the
JSVariable form is two. So the widening is the removal of a conjunct.

Removing it exposed three defects the conjunct had been masking, two of them
wrong answers. The numeric tier is sound because a name referenced before its
declaration is refused, which makes text order imply execution order; capture
breaks that link in two places.

  * A function declaration at body top level exists before the body runs, so
    `var r = g(); var s = 0; function g() { return s; }` reads s before its
    initializer from a position the analysis accepts. Returned 0 for
    undefined. Fixed by a conjunct that is deliberately NOT behind the switch,
    because it is correctness: a name a body-top-level declaration mentions
    keeps its cell. Only body-top-level ones qualify — a declaration in a
    block has its binding hoisted but not its value.
  * A declaration inside a nested function is a different binding, so letting
    it open `declared` masked a read of the outer name that really does see
    undefined. Returned "0,5" for "undefined,5". Writes stay conflated at
    every depth, which is what drops a candidate a closure assigns a string.
  * A function declaration stores a function object into the binding being
    typed, and a declaration is not an assignment expression, so the walk
    never saw the store. `let f = 5; { function f() {} }` failed to compile
    ("Assignment target Call is not supported").

Worth 8 names on the seven Octane suites, 224 -> 232, not the 290 the item was
sized at: 247 of its 478 captured names are named by a hoisted declaration and
are closed permanently, and the 290 was inferred from offered-minus-dropped
when Resolve removes a third population between those counters that had no
counter at all. Adds it, so offered = rejected + dropped + surviving
reconciles: 2 521 = 359 + 1 916 + 246.

On its shape: 63.97 -> 0.01 B/iter, -112 B an activation, and shape/control
7.19x -> 1.0000x. Losing side, equally exact: +32 B and 1.111x when the value
is read through the closure. On the corpus 1.0001x. Off with
BROILER_JS_CAPTURED_NUMERIC_LOCALS=0.

CapturedNumericLocalTests is 24 cases, each asserted on both settings of the
switch; the probes they were written from answer identically on a pristine
build of the pinned pointer.
